From 6e01542241c4e80f8e19fba3b5de46c62489753a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: akapug <akapug@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 16:49:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/16] sycl: small-batch quantized mat-mul on the XMX engines
 (Q5_K)

At 2-8 columns the dp4a mat-vec kernels do their int8 math on the vector units and turn
compute bound: on an Arc Pro B70 a Q5_K 5120x17408 weight takes 108 us at 1 column, 235 at 4
and 415 at 8, against a ~104 us memory floor. Serve 3-8 columns on the XMX engines instead.
Each sub-group owns 16 weight rows and dequantizes each 256-value block of the reorder layout
to fp16 straight into the B operand registers (a lane holds its own row). The activations are
converted once to fp16, padded to 8 rows and stored (k0, k2, k1, k3) per 4, so one nibble mask
of a weight word yields an fp16 pair in operand order. K is split over work-groups when there
are too few row groups to fill the card, and a second kernel sums the splits.

The fused gate/up + GLU dp4a path declines at 4-8 columns when both weights can take this
path; at 3 the fusion still wins. GGML_SYCL_XMX_MMVQ=0 turns the path off.

Arc Pro B70, test-backend-ops perf, Q5_K, dp4a -> XMX (us): 5120x17408 n3 188 -> 146,
n4 235 -> 147, n8 415 -> 150; 17408x5120 n4 186 -> 157, n8 373 -> 162; 5120x6144 n4 72 -> 40.
1 and 2 columns unchanged. Qwen3-4B with all-Q5_K FFN weights, llama-batched-bench decode:
4 sequences +7.6%, 8 sequences +26%, 1-3 unchanged. test-backend-ops MUL_MAT and
MUL_MAT_VEC_FUSION pass against the CPU; adds cases for one and several K splits, alone and
as SWIGLU/GEGLU FFNs at 3, 4 and 8 tokens.
